.\" zhen.1 — the manual page.
.\"
.\" Written against the binary rather than against the source: every exit code below
.\" was produced by running the command in the SYNOPSIS, and every capability name is
.\" one the artifact actually emits. A manual that documents intentions is how a tool
.\" comes to claim things it does not do.
.\"
.\" Checked against `Zhen.configuration.subcommands` and each command's `help` output,
.\" not against memory: every option the parser accepts is here, and nothing is here
.\" that it does not accept. There is no `--udp` flag; UDP is `U:` in a port spec.
.Dd October 16, 2026
.Dt ZHEN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zhen
.Nd fast native network discovery
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm scan
.Op Fl -cidr Ar network
.Op Fl -iface Ar name
.Op Fl -deadline Ar duration
.Op Fl -concurrency Ar n
.Op Fl -discovery-only
.Op Fl -deep Op Fl -ports Ar spec
.Op Fl -scan-type Ar type
.Op Fl -ports-view Ar view
.Op Fl -yes
.Op Fl -retransmit-timeout Ar duration
.Op Fl -shared-budget
.Op Fl -reach Ar policy
.Op Fl -reach-percentile Ar p
.Op Fl -no-neighbor-watch
.Op Fl -look-longer
.Op Fl -first-scan-wait Ar duration
.Op Fl -reach-cap Ar duration
.Op Fl -reach-min-samples Ar n
.Op Fl -expected-attempts-step Ar duration
.Op Fl -expected-attempts-cap Ar n
.Op Fl -expected-window Ar duration
.Op Fl -unexpected-cold Ar rule
.Op Fl -no-history
.Op Fl -json | Fl -ndjson
.Op Fl -quiet
.Op Fl -no-color
.Nm
.Cm ports
.Ar targets
.Op Ar spec
.Op Fl -ports Ar spec
.Op Fl -deadline Ar duration
.Op Fl -scan-type Ar type
.Op Fl -yes
.Op Fl -check
.Op Fl -record
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -quiet
.Op Fl -no-color
.Nm
.Cm hosts
.Op Fl -store Ar path
.Op Fl -since Ar duration
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Cm events
.Op Fl -store Ar path
.Op Fl -since Ar duration
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Cm history Ar host
.Op Fl -store Ar path
.Op Fl -since Ar duration
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Cm export
.Op Fl -store Ar path
.Op Fl -since Ar duration
.Op Fl -json
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
finds the devices on a local segment, identifies them, and remembers
what it has seen so it can say what changed.
It opens no subprocesses, bundles no interpreters and depends on no external
binaries: every protocol it speaks is implemented in the tool.
.Pp
Discovery sweeps the IPv4 network and, beside it, asks the IPv6 link: one echo
request to the all-nodes group
.Li ff02::1 ,
and a read of the kernel's IPv6 neighbour cache.
A host found at a v4 address and at link-local v6 ones is reported as one
device, joined by its hardware address.
.Pp
It runs fully without privileges, and that is the default rather than a
fallback.
Run under
.Xr sudo 8
it additionally opens a packet-capture device and adds a layer-2 ARP sweep,
half-open port scanning and TCP stack fingerprinting.
.Ss Commands
.Bl -tag -width "history host"
.It Cm scan
Discover hosts on the network, name them, and optionally probe their ports.
The default command.
.It Cm ports
Probe TCP and UDP ports on hosts you already have, IPv4 or IPv6.
Discovery is never run, so a host that answers no ping is still scannable by
address.
.It Cm hosts
List every device the history has ever seen, present or absent.
.It Cm events
Show what changed between scans, newest first.
.It Cm history Ar host
Show one device: what is known about it, and what has happened to it.
.Ar host
is an address, a MAC, or part of a name.
.It Cm export
Write the whole history to standard output as one JSON document.
.El
.Ss Scan options
.Bl -tag -width "--retransmit-timeout d"
.It Fl -cidr Ar network
Network to scan, in CIDR form.
Defaults to the network of the interface the default route uses.
Anything wider than a
.Pa /16
is refused rather than swept, because a sweep that cannot finish reports an
empty network instead of an unfinished one.
.It Fl -iface Ar name
Interface to scan from.
Defaults to the one the default route uses, which is the only answer that is
right on a machine with a VPN, a container bridge and a tethered phone all up
at once.
.It Fl -deadline Ar duration
Total budget, for example
.Ar 5s ,
.Ar 500ms
or
.Ar 2m .
Defaults to
.Ar 20s .
Spending it is not a failure: see
.Sx EXIT STATUS .
.It Fl -concurrency Ar n
Probes in flight.
Derived from the descriptor limit the kernel actually granted when omitted, and
clamped rather than rejected when it exceeds what that limit supports.
.It Fl -discovery-only
Find hosts and stop.
No naming, no ports.
.It Fl -deep
Also probe TCP ports on the hosts that answered, and ask the open ones what they
are.
Only their IPv4 addresses are probed; use
.Cm ports
for an IPv6 address.
.It Fl -ports Ar spec
Ports for
.Fl -deep ,
in the grammar under
.Sx Port specs .
TCP only: a
.Li U:\&
entry is refused with the
.Cm ports
command that would scan it.
Defaults to
.Li common
rather than all 65,535, because a deep scan is a
deliberate act and sixteen million probes across a
.Pa /24
is not a default.
.It Fl -scan-type Ar type
How ports are probed:
.Li syn ,
.Li connect
or
.Li auto ,
the default.
See
.Sx Scan types .
.It Fl -ports-view Ar view
How the port table is drawn:
.Li matrix
(hosts by ports),
.Li long
(one row per open port), or
.Li auto ,
the default, which picks by shape.
.It Fl -yes
Confirm a deep scan large enough to be refused without it.
.It Fl -retransmit-timeout Ar duration
Pin how long an unanswered probe waits before being resent, for example
.Ar 150ms .
Normally measured from the network and re-derived as replies arrive; pinning it
stops the scan adapting, which exists to measure what the adapting is worth.
The artifact records that the interval was imposed.
.It Fl -shared-budget
Use one budget for both retransmitting and declaring hosts absent, as the scan
did before the two were separated.
Slower by construction; exists so the split can be measured against its absence.
.It Fl -reach Ar policy
How long to wait for each address:
.Li history ,
the default,
.Li learned ,
or a duration such as
.Ar 3s .
Under
.Li history
an address this network's history says usually answers, and that has no
neighbour entry when the scan starts, is waited for up to a high percentile of
how long such addresses took to answer when nothing was cached, capped at 5 s,
and asked more often.
One that already has a complete entry is reported present from it, and the
scan does not wait for it.
Every other address gets the short wait learned from the scan's own replies.
A network with fewer than 20 uncached times waits 1.5 s for an expected address
with no entry.
Until the network is known, that is, until its history holds 20 times of either
kind, it waits 1.5 s for every address with no entry, and that scan is what
makes the next one fast.
An address with a complete entry never waits it, and on a known network neither
does an address history does not expect
.Pq Fl -unexpected-cold .
.Li learned
is the short wait for every address, which is how scans worked before 0.8.0.
A duration waits exactly that long for every address.
The artifact records what was used as
.Li run.reach .
.It Fl -reach-percentile Ar p
With
.Fl -reach Li history
only: the percentile, between 0 and 1, of past first-contact times to wait for.
Defaults to 0.97.
.It Fl -no-neighbor-watch
Do not watch the neighbour table while sweeping.
Normally an address that resolves during the sweep is reported at that moment, as
.Li neighbor_resolved ;
without the watch the table is read only before and after, and every host only the
table supplied is
.Li neighbor_table .
An ablation: with
.Fl -reach Li learned
it is the mechanism zhen 0.7.0 used.
.It Fl -look-longer
Ask again, for longer, about the devices the latest scan expected and did not find
.Pq its Li expected_missing ,
waiting 10 s for each or
.Fl -reach Ar duration .
What answers is added to that scan rather than starting a new one, so nothing else
on the network is marked gone.
It asks on the interface that scan went out of;
.Fl -iface
naming a different one is refused.
The artifact names the scan it extended in
.Li run.extends_run .
.It Fl -first-scan-wait Ar duration
How long an address with no neighbour entry waits on a network with too little
uncached history, if the scan expects it or the network is not known yet.
Defaults to
.Ar 1.5s .
.It Fl -reach-cap Ar duration
The longest any expected address waits, however slow its history.
Defaults to
.Ar 5s .
.It Fl -reach-min-samples Ar n
How many first-contact times of a kind, uncached or cached, a network needs
before they decide the waits of the addresses they describe.
Defaults to 20.
.It Fl -expected-attempts-step Ar duration
Each whole step of wait beyond the first earns an address one more transmission.
Defaults to
.Ar 500ms .
.It Fl -expected-attempts-cap Ar n
The most transmissions any address gets.
Defaults to 8.
.It Fl -expected-window Ar duration
How recently a device must have been seen at an address for the scan to expect
it there.
History is kept for ever; this is how far back it counts.
Defaults to
.Ar 30d .
.It Fl -unexpected-cold Ar rule
What an address history does not expect, with no neighbour entry, waits on a
known network:
.Li learned ,
the default, or
.Li first-scan .
A network is known once its history holds as many first-contact times, uncached
or cached, as
.Fl -reach-min-samples .
While it has fewer uncached ones than that,
.Li learned
gives those addresses the short learned wait, and
.Li first-scan
gives them the first-scan wait.
An expected address, and every address on a network that is not known yet, waits
the first-scan wait either way.
.Li run.reach.known_network
says whether the network was known.
.It Fl -no-history
Neither read nor write history for this scan: it runs as a first scan and leaves
no trace.
.Ev ZHEN_HISTORY
points a scan at a different history file instead.
.Pp
These eight exist so the waits can be measured from outside a frozen build.
Every value a scan used, set by a flag or not, is in
.Li run.reach .
.It Fl -json
Emit one scan report as JSON on standard output.
.It Fl -ndjson
Emit one event per line as JSON on standard output, as they happen.
.It Fl -quiet
Suppress the progress display on standard error.
.It Fl -no-color
Disable ANSI colour.
Colour is already disabled automatically when standard output is not a terminal.
.El
.Ss Ports
.Cm ports
takes
.Ar targets
first and the port
.Ar spec
second:
.Dl $ zhen ports 10.10.20.1 22
.Pp
.Ar targets
is one address, a comma list, an IPv4 CIDR of at most 1024 addresses, or
.Li -
to read addresses from standard input, one per line.
IPv4 and IPv6 mix freely in one list and are reported in the order given.
A link-local IPv6 address needs its interface after
.Li % ,
as in
.Li fe80::1%en0 ,
because the same address exists once per link; one without is refused, as is a
zone naming no interface on this machine, and an IPv6 prefix, which is not an
enumeration.
.Pp
.Ar spec
defaults to
.Li common .
.Fl -ports Ar spec
is the same thing as a flag; giving both, differently, is refused.
.Bl -tag -width "--deadline duration"
.It Fl -deadline Ar duration
Total budget.
Defaults to
.Ar 20s .
.It Fl -scan-type Ar type
As for
.Cm scan ;
see
.Sx Scan types .
.It Fl -yes
Confirm a scan large enough to be refused without it.
.It Fl -check
Print nothing, and answer with the exit status: 0 if any requested port is
.Li open ,
1 if none is.
Behaves identically for IPv4 and IPv6 targets.
.Li open_filtered
counts as not open, because a shell
.Ic if
cannot represent
.Dq maybe .
.It Fl -record
Keep the results in history: the ports of each device history already holds,
and an event for each port that opened or stopped being open.
Never recorded as a scan, so nothing goes offline because it was not asked
about, and a host history has never seen is skipped rather than added.
Written where
.Cm scan
writes, so
.Ev ZHEN_HISTORY
moves it too.
.It Fl -json
Emit one port-scan report as JSON on standard output.
.It Fl -quiet , Fl -no-color
As for
.Cm scan .
.El
.Ss Port specs
Comma separated; every entry is one of:
.Bl -tag -width "1-1024,!135-139" -compact
.It Li 22
one port
.It Li 1-1024
an inclusive range
.It Li common
this project's set of 36 commonly served ports
.It Li iana
IANA's assigned ports in 1\(en1023
.It Li all
1\(en65535
.It Li domain
an IANA service name selects its port.
A name selects; it never names: a port chosen as
.Li domain
reports no service unless something on the wire says what it is.
.It Li !135-139
excludes, after everything else is included
.It Li U:53
UDP.
.Li T:\&
or no prefix is TCP.
.El
.Ss Port states
.Bl -tag -width "open_filtered" -compact
.It Li open
Something accepted the connection, or answered the datagram.
.It Li closed
The host refused: a TCP reset, or for UDP an ICMP port-unreachable.
.It Li filtered
TCP: nothing answered.
UDP: an ICMP error other than port-unreachable, such as a host or
address unreachable.
.It Li open_filtered
UDP only: silence.
An open port ignoring a request it did not understand and a firewall dropping it
look the same from here, and the tool says so rather than guess.
.El
.Pp
Each observation carries the address it was made on, so the same port on a
device's v4 and v6 addresses is two rows and may disagree.
.Ss Scan types
.Bl -tag -width "connect"
.It Li connect
A full TCP connection, and UDP through a connected socket.
Needs no privilege.
IPv4 and IPv6.
.It Li syn
Half-open: one SYN, and the reply read from a capture device.
Needs one, and is refused rather than quietly replaced by
.Li connect
when there is none.
.Em IPv4 only :
an IPv6 target with
.Fl -scan-type Li syn
is refused by name before anything is sent, and exits 64.
.It Li auto
.Li syn
where a capture device is available and
.Li connect
where it is not.
With a capture device, IPv6 targets are sent to
.Li connect ,
because choosing is what
.Li auto
means.
.El
.Pp
UDP ports always use the unprivileged UDP engine, whatever the scan type.
Service identification from banners and certificates reaches IPv4 ports only; an
open IPv6 port is reported without a service, and the report says it was not
asked.
.Ss History options
For
.Cm hosts ,
.Cm events ,
.Cm history
and
.Cm export :
.Bl -tag -width "--since duration"
.It Fl -store Ar path
Read this history file instead of the default one.
.It Fl -since Ar duration
Only entries newer than this, for example
.Ar 24h
or
.Ar 7d ,
measured back from now rather than from the last scan.
.It Fl -json
Emit JSON on standard output instead of a table.
.El
.Sh PRIVILEGES
.Nm
never prompts for a password and never spawns
.Xr sudo 8 .
What it does is attempt to open a packet-capture device, and compose the engines
that attempt succeeds for.
.Pp
Selection is by attempt rather than by checking the effective user id, because
that check is wrong in both directions: root with every
.Pa /dev/bpf*
node held by
.Xr tcpdump 1 ,
Wireshark or a VPN client has exactly the capability an ordinary user has, and a
machine running a ChmodBPF-style daemon gives an ordinary user the capability
root would have.
.Pp
Running as root never finds
.Em fewer
hosts than running without it.
The privileged and unprivileged discovery engines are composed and their results
unioned, because an either/or would lose every host that answers ICMP but not
ARP.
.Pp
The capabilities each run actually exercised are reported in the artifact under
.Li run.engine.capabilities :
.Bl -tag -width "neighbor_table" -compact
.It Li icmp_sweep
Unprivileged ICMP echo sweep.
.It Li neighbor_table
The kernel's neighbour table, read as a discovery source.
.It Li arp_sweep
Layer-2 ARP requests this tool put on the wire itself.
Needs a capture device.
.It Li connect_scan
Full TCP connect port scanning.
.It Li udp_scan
UDP port scanning.
.It Li syn_scan
Half-open port scanning.
Needs a capture device.
.It Li banner_probe
Service identification from a banner, an HTTP
.Li Server
header or a TLS certificate subject.
.It Li os_fingerprint
TCP stack fingerprinting from a SYN-ACK's options.
Needs a capture device.
.El
.Pp
A capability appears there only when it was
.Em used ,
never merely because the engine offers it.
.Pp
Each device in the artifact also carries
.Li discovered_via ,
which says how that host was found rather than only that it was:
.Bl -tag -width "neighbor_resolved" -compact
.It Li probe_reply
It answered an echo request this scan sent: the ICMP sweep, or for an IPv6
address the
.Li ff02::1
solicitation.
.It Li arp_reply
It answered an ARP request this tool put on the wire.
.It Li neighbor_resolved
Its neighbour entry resolved while the scan was running: the kernel's ARP, asked
because of this scan's probe, was answered.
It did not answer the echo in time, or at all.
.It Li neighbor_table
The kernel's neighbour table listed it, ARP or IPv6, before the scan started,
and it did not answer in time.
A host whose reply arrives after the sweep has given up is found this way, so
a run with several of these is also saying its per-host budgets are tight.
A host that ignores ICMP is found this way and is genuinely present, but
.Em no frame from it will appear in a packet capture ,
which is the difference this field exists to record.
An IPv6 cache entry can outlive its host by hours.
.It Li local_interface
This machine's own address.
A probe a host sends to itself is answered inside the kernel and never
reaches the wire, so a capture cannot show it either.
.El
.Pp
Reconciling a scan against
.Xr tcpdump 1
means comparing the wire only against the hosts marked
.Li probe_reply
or
.Li arp_reply .
The others are expected to be missing from it.
.Pp
On a device with several addresses
.Li discovered_via
is the union over all of them, so a dual-stack host can be
.Li neighbor_resolved
on its IPv4 address and
.Li neighbor_table
on its link-local one at once.
.Li address_sources
lists each address with its own
.Li discovered_via .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "ZHEN_HISTORY"
.It Ev ZHEN_HISTORY
Path to the history file, honoured exactly: no directory is appended and no
extension is assumed.
Set it to keep a scan out of the real history, or to compare two histories
side by side.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/Library/Application Support/zhen/"
.It Pa ~/Library/Application Support/zhen/history.ndjson
The append-only history every scan is recorded into.
.Pp
Under
.Xr sudo 8
this is still the invoking user's path, not
.Pa /var/root .
A privileged scan that wrote somewhere only root can read would give the same
machine two histories that never meet, and the one the user could see would have
holes in it exactly where the interesting scans were.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Every status below was produced by running the command beside it.
.Bl -tag -width "69"
.It 0
The scan ran.
This includes a partial result from an expired deadline: whether a run was
exhaustive is the
.Li run.outcome
field in the artifact, not the exit status, and a job that treated a busy
network as a failure would go red every time somebody streamed a film.
.Cm ports
with nothing open is also 0: an answer, not a failure.
With
.Fl -check ,
0 means at least one requested port is open.
.It 1
.Cm ports Fl -check
only: the scan ran and no requested port is open.
.It 64
Usage error.
An unparseable
.Fl -cidr ,
a network too wide to sweep, an unknown subcommand; for
.Cm ports ,
an address that is neither IPv4 nor IPv6, a link-local address without its
interface, an interface that does not exist, or
.Fl -scan-type Li syn
with an IPv6 target.
.It 69
A required capability was unavailable: no such interface, no usable interface,
a platform with no scanning engine, or
.Fl -scan-type Li syn
without a capture device.
Also returned when a scan completed but recorded a degradation it could not
recover from: a denied Local Network permission, most often, which returns zero
results rather than an error and would otherwise be indistinguishable from an
empty network.
.It 75
A temporary failure: a syscall failed for a reason that may not recur.
.It 130
Interrupted.
The first
.Dv SIGINT
commits what has been gathered and exits; the results are partial and real.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Discover what is on this network and print a table:
.Pp
.Dl $ zhen scan
.Pp
A machine-readable report, with no progress display:
.Pp
.Dl $ zhen scan --json --quiet > report.json
.Pp
Fail a CI job when the Local Network permission is denied, but not when the
network is merely busy:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zhen scan --discovery-only --quiet --json > report.json
case $? in
  0)  echo "scanned" ;;
  69) echo "permission or interface problem"; exit 1 ;;
  *)  echo "usage or transient failure"; exit 1 ;;
esac
.Ed
.Pp
Everything the privileged path adds, with the ports that matter on this segment:
.Pp
.Dl $ sudo zhen scan --deep --ports 22,80,443 --json
.Pp
Watch hosts appear as they answer, one JSON object per line:
.Pp
.Dl $ zhen scan --ndjson | jq -c 'select(.event == "host_found")'
.Pp
Is 22 open on the gateway?
.Pp
.Dl $ zhen ports 10.10.20.1 22
.Pp
The same question of a link-local IPv6 address, and of both families in one
report, with UDP:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zhen ports fe80::82f:e557:372d:c208%en0 22,7000
zhen ports 10.10.20.1,fe80::82f:e557:372d:c208%en0 22,80,U:53,U:5353
.Ed
.Pp
Connect only if the port is open:
.Pp
.Dl $ if zhen ports "$HOST" 22 --check; then ssh "$HOST"; fi
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tcpdump 1 ,
.Xr arp 8 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8 ,
.Xr ndp 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Local Network permission on current macOS is denied
.Em silently :
a denied process gets zero results rather than an error, and a bare command-line
binary has no bundle, so the grant attaches to the terminal rather than
appearing under this tool's own name in System Settings.
.Nm
detects the signature of a denial, which is probes hearing nothing while the
neighbour table is populated, and reports it as a degradation.
It cannot query the grant, so that detection is a heuristic and says so where it
appears.
.Pp
Stack fingerprinting matches against a deliberately small table of stack
lineages and names a family rather than a version.
A host it cannot place reports the features it observed with no name attached.
That is the honest outcome, and the one that lets a signature be written later
against reports already collected.
.Pp
One echo to
.Li ff02::1
does not reach every IPv6 host: a stack that ignores multicast echo stays
silent, and on the network this was measured on, four hosts in ten did.
It complements the IPv4 sweep; it does not replace it.
.Pp
IPv6 is link-local and scanned by address: there is no IPv6 sweep, no
half-open IPv6 scan, and no service identification on IPv6 ports.
Routed topologies and networks wider than a
.Pa /16
are out of scope.
